  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Monday)\nIssued at 1127 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nA broad area of surface high pressure centered over the eastern\nCONUS will continue to bring quiet weather for the next several\ndays. Temperatures will gradually warm through the week. Toward \nthe end of the week, the ridge shifts far enough east for our area\nto get more of a southerly flow and some moisture return. An upper\nstorm system is expected to lift out of the Southwest and into the\ncentral and northern Plains this weekend. Surface low pressure\nwill track from near Denver to the northern Great Lakes, with a\ntrailing cold front entering our area Saturday afternoon or night.\nThere will be a chance for showers and maybe a few storms with the\nfront as it moves through. The trend in the data has been toward\nlesser coverage of rain and storms and thus lower PoPs and thunder\nprobs, owing to moisture quality issues and the fact that the \nbulk of the upper energy with the system will pass well to our \nnorth.\n\nThe 00Z EC and 12Z GFS suggest more active weather is possible\nduring the first part of the holiday week, with a storm system \ntracking across the southern tier of states.\n\nLacy",
